WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.935
3 minutes of terror.

00:00:01.935 --> 00:00:04.170
Four passengers on an Asiana Airlines

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.372
flight in South Korea today.

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:06.439
Investigators.

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:06.973
They're now

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.808
honestly trying to figure out

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:09.609
how it happened,

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:10.410
how a man

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.678
seemingly was able

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.747
to open the aircraft door

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:16.516
of this plane mid-flight.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.718
This was as the plane

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.487
also was about to land.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:21.254
Watch

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.466
You can hear the wind

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:35.168
just whipping through,

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.603
ripping through the cabin.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:38.138
You can see the passengers there

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:39.606
gripping their seats.

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:41.341
Also, which is oddly silent

00:00:41.341 --> 00:00:42.876
but understandably silent

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.344
because of how terrifying

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:44.911
you can imagine.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.146
This is an official

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:47.280
for the airline said that

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.215
a man in the exit row appeared

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:49.816
to try

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.418
appeared to open the door

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:53.920
while the plane was 700 feet in the air.

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.522
One passenger also said

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:56.089
that she believed

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:56.956
the man was trying to get

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.158
off the aircraft

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.926
and fellow passengers helped the crew

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:01.928
pull him back from the opening.

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:02.729
Let me bring in

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:03.663
CNN aviation

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:06.032
analyst Miles O'Brien for more on this.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:06.199
Right,

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:08.401
Miles, on on several levels,

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.003
this should not have happened

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.704
and should not have been able to happen.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:12.872
What do you see here?

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:15.542
Well, Kate, the

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:16.309
key thing

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:16.976
to know is

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.045
these doors are like stoppers

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.080
in a bathtub there.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.115
They're plugged doors, they're called.

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.317
And when the plane is pressurized

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.554
at altitude, it is humanly impossible.

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:29.789
To pull them in and push them out.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.291
They are sealed tight

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:32.759
like that stopper in the bathtub.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:34.461
But when you get lower

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:36.062
and the pressure is equalized,

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.531
which it would be at 700 feet.

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.301
That possibility, it becomes

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.635
it becomes a possibility

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:43.703
for somebody to do this.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.705
So it happened.

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:49.109
Fortunately, nobody got hurt And it's

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:52.445
really part of a string of cases

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.746
of passengers

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:54.747
that have been trying to monkey

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.049
around with these doors.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:56.783
That's a good point.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:58.751
I mean, and to your point,

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:00.353
they said that there are 200 people

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:01.187
on board.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:01.888
12 people.

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.022
They suffered

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.091
how it's described as minor injuries

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:06.693
like hyperventilation.

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:07.760
Are you surprised?

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.196
Is it surprising that this wasn't worse,

00:02:10.196 --> 00:02:11.264
considering the details

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:11.898
that you're learning

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:14.400
now about where they were when this was?

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:17.904
I you know, I think

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:18.471
the pilots

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:19.439
had certainly

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:20.673
a distraction on short

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.408
final as they are about to land.

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:23.676
All kinds of warning

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.612
signs would have been indicated

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:26.713
on that flight. Deck.

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:28.181
So the fact that they landed it out

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.083
eventually had tip to the crew

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:31.217
and frankly,

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:31.618
a hat

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:33.286
tip to the passengers on board

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:34.954
who saw a problem

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:36.556
and neutralized it, tackled them.

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.258
It's good to see that, actually.

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:40.026
So to put a fine point

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.127
on what you're talking about,

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.296
this was about two to 3 minutes

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:44.497
from landing there,

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.766
about 700 feet above the ground

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.568
when the door was opened.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.603
If they were at higher altitude,

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.138
this would not have been possible.

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:53.740
This wasn't a mechanical error.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.108
You don't think in your mind

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:55.742
this was just

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.577
this was just sheer physics,

00:02:57.577 --> 00:02:59.312
that this was possible to happen,

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:01.447
whether that

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.282
person knew

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.651
that and waited to be that low?

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.119
I cannot say of course.

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:07.420
But sheer physics.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:07.887
Well,

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.556
I don't want this to be the takeaway

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:11.524
for everybody else. They're flying.

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:12.492
This can't happen

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.093
once the aircraft is pressurized

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.361
because as it is pressurized,

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:16.429
at altitude,

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.498
it pushes those doors in like a plug

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.700
and you can't pull them open

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.135
no matter how hard you try.

00:03:22.135 --> 00:03:24.137
So that's the good news there.

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:25.438
This incident happened

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:26.706
when the pressure was equalized

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.042
so you could open the door.

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.443
So I guess, you know, that

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:31.177
that boils down

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:32.512
to passengers

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:33.613
being very attentive

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.014
of their fellow passengers.

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.449
Which it looks like is

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:37.717
what happened here.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:39.285
Absolutely

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:40.787
comforting hearing you're saying.

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:41.688
And also terrifying.

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:42.622
Still seeing this video

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:42.855
of what

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.457
these passengers were experiencing

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.359
in the last 3 minutes of their flight.

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:47.493
For sure.

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:48.228
It's great to see you.

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:50.163
Miles, thank you very much for coming in.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:51.731
CNN's Tom Foreman joins me now.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:52.732
So, Tom, one,

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.267
I didn't know this was possible.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.635
Experts say it shouldn't

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:57.770
have been possible saying all that.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:58.571
How did this happen?

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.208
It's not supposed to be possible.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:03.176
The simple truth is

00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:04.777
when a plane is flying

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.013
the pressure inside the plane

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:09.048
and the fact the doors are locked

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:10.617
are supposed to make it impossible

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:12.185
for you to open that door.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:13.253
The door would be so heavy,

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.054
it would be like trying to lift a car

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.257
to open it in full flight.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.625
But you mentioned a key thing.

00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:19.225
There were.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.961
This plane was only about 700 feet

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:23.029
above the ground,

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:24.063
according to authorities.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:25.531
When this happened.

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.667
It was only two or 3 minutes away

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.602
from actually touching down.

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:30.770
So arguably

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.305
at that altitude,

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:33.006
the pressure, well,

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:35.041
not arguably factually at that pressure.

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:37.010
At that altitude, the pressure is much,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:37.410
much, much,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.244
much lower, really

00:04:38.244 --> 00:04:39.712
about the same pressure you'd have

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:41.648
at the top of a 60 story building.

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:43.449
So no real pressure at all.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.118
Still real questions about how we got it

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:45.718
open here

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:46.653
especially considering

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.721
that there also a landing speed issue.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:50.056
You'd be going about 100

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:52.292
and 772 miles an hour

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.493
when you're landing at this

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.728
that also could have posed a problem

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:56.763
nonetheless.

00:04:56.763 --> 00:04:58.831
Passengers say this guy jumped up

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:00.033
started yanking at the door

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.801
trying to get it open in some fashion.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:02.502
Flight attendant

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:03.970
sort of yelling help, help

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.505
And about ten people rushed forward

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.106
and they pulled him back down.

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:08.941
And then they had a very breezy landing

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:10.143
from that point on.

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.944
I mean, so many questions.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.713
Tom, what do we know about the passengers

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.782
who were aboard this flight?

00:05:16.149 --> 00:05:17.216
What were the extent

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.418
of injuries, if at all?

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:20.420
Nobody seems to have

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.654
been seriously injured.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.323
There were like a dozen people

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:25.358
who suffered sort of hyperventilation.

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:26.592
Some people may hyperventilate

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.628
just hearing about this story.

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:30.029
And this has been tried before.

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:31.631
This is not the first incident

00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:32.699
where people have tried

00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:35.802
to open a airplane door in flight,

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:37.303
but guaranteed in this case,

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:38.171
there will be a lot of

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.206
looks at the construction of this plane

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:42.809
and why even at a low altitude,

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:44.644
he was able to get the door open.

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.779
It's supposed to be impossible

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:47.780
like the Celtics

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.682
winning the NBA championship.

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:51.551
So we'll have to see what happens.

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:53.319
It's supposed to be Tom Foreman

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:54.087
Thank you, my friend.

